<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-undetermined-dup-example" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Example with the Case of Duplication</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE: REMIND OF CASE OF DUPLICATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Recall from <xref ref="thm-case-of-duplication"/> that when the ODE has the form
    <me>y'' + k^2 y = m_1\cos(kt) + m_2\sin(kt),</me>
    the usual guess <m>y_p = A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt)</m> fails because it solves
    the homogeneous equation. In this case we use the modified guess
    <me>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt)\bigr).</me>
    Let's work through a full example.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="example-dup-full">
    <title>Finding a Particular Solution in the Case of Duplication</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Find a particular solution to
        <me>y'' + 9y = 12\cos(3t) + 16\sin(3t).</me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ── Part (a): Recognize case of duplication, MC ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-dup-ex-recognize">
      <title>Recognizing the case of duplication</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Notice that the coefficient of <m>y</m> is <m>9</m>, and the
          right-hand side has frequency <m>k = 3</m>. Since <m>k^2 = 9</m>
          matches the coefficient of <m>y</m>, we are in the case of duplication.
          What is the correct guess for <m>y_p</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Since we are in the case of duplication, we multiply
              the usual guess by <m>t</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This is the usual guess, but we just established we are in the
              case of duplication. This guess solves <m>y'' + 9y = 0</m>,
              so substituting it in gives <m>0</m> on the left, not
              <m>12\cos(3t) + 16\sin(3t)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(9t) + B\sin(9t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The frequency in the guess should match the right-hand side,
              which has <m>k = 3</m>, not <m>k = 9</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t^2\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Multiplying by <m>t</m> is enough to fix the duplication.
              We only need <m>t^2</m> in more advanced cases not covered here.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Part (b): Work it out ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-dup-ex-solve">
      <title>Finding <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the guess <m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m>.
          Substitute into <m>y'' + 9y = 12\cos(3t) + 16\sin(3t)</m> and
          solve for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Using the product rule:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>y_p' = \bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr) + t\bigl(-3A\sin(3t) + 3B\cos(3t)\bigr)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>y_p'' = \bigl(6B\cos(3t) - 6A\sin(3t)\bigr) + t\bigl(-9A\cos(3t) - 9B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          Plug these into <m>y'' + 9y</m> and solve for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>y_p' = \bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr) + t\bigl(-3A\sin(3t) + 3B\cos(3t)\bigr)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>y_p'' = ?? </m></p></li>
          </ul>
          <solution>
            <title>Computing <m>y_p''</m> via product rule</title>
            <p>
              Differentiating <m>y_p'</m> with the product rule:
              <md>
                <mrow>y_p'' \amp= \bigl(-3A\sin(3t) + 3B\cos(3t)\bigr)</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp\quad + \bigl(-3A\sin(3t) + 3B\cos(3t)\bigr)</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp\quad + t\bigl(-9A\cos(3t) - 9B\sin(3t)\bigr)</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp= \bigl(6B\cos(3t) - 6A\sin(3t)\bigr) + t\bigl(-9A\cos(3t) - 9B\sin(3t)\bigr).</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </solution>
          So:
          <me>y_p'' = \underbrace{\bigl(6B\cos(3t) - 6A\sin(3t)\bigr)}_{\text{terms without }t} + \textcolor{blue}{\underbrace{t\bigl(-9A\cos(3t) - 9B\sin(3t)\bigr)}_{\text{terms with }t}}.</me>
          Now substituting into <m>y'' + 9y</m>:
          <solution>
            <title>Substituting and cancelling the <m>t</m> terms</title>
            <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>y_p'' + 9y_p
                  \amp= \underbrace{6B\cos(3t) - 6A\sin(3t)}_{\text{terms from }y_p''\text{ without }t}
                  + \textcolor{blue}{\underbrace{t(-9A\cos(3t) - 9B\sin(3t))}_{\text{terms from }y_p''\text{ with }t}}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp\quad + \textcolor{blue}{\underbrace{9t(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t))}_{\text{all terms from }9y_p}}.</mrow>
              </md>
              The <m>\textcolor{blue}{t}</m> terms cancel:
              <me>\textcolor{blue}{-9At\cos(3t) - 9Bt\sin(3t) + 9At\cos(3t) + 9Bt\sin(3t) = 0.}</me>
            </p>
          </solution>
          After cancellation, our ODE becomes:
          <me>\underbrace{6B\cos(3t) - 6A\sin(3t)}_{y_p''+9y_p \text{ after cancellation}} = \underbrace{12\cos(3t) + 16\sin(3t)}_{\text{original RHS}}.</me>
          Now we solve for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>:
          <solution>
            <title>Solving for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></title>
            <p>
              Matching coefficients in
              <me>\textcolor{red}{6B\cos(3t)} \textcolor{blue}{- 6A\sin(3t)} = \textcolor{red}{12\cos(3t)} + \textcolor{blue}{16\sin(3t)}:</me>
              <md>
                <mrow>\textcolor{blue}{-6A} \amp\textcolor{blue}{= 16} \amp\implies A \amp= -\frac{8}{3}</mrow>
                <mrow>\textcolor{red}{6B} \amp\textcolor{red}{= 12} \amp\implies B \amp= 2</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </solution>
          So <m>A = -\dfrac{8}{3}</m> and <m>B = 2</m>, giving the particular solution:
          <me>y_p = t\!\left(\underbrace{-\frac{8}{3}}_{A}\cos(3t) + \underbrace{2}_{B}\sin(3t)\right).</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: T TERMS ALWAYS CANCEL
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Notice what happened with the <m>t</m> terms when we substituted in.
    In this example, the terms with <m>t</m> were
    <m>t(-9A\cos(3t) - 9B\sin(3t))</m> from <m>y_p''</m> and
    <m>9t(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t))</m> from <m>9y_p</m>. These cancelled
    exactly because <m>A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)</m> solves the homogeneous
    equation <m>y'' + 9y = 0</m>, which is precisely the definition of
    the case of duplication. This is not a coincidence: the <m>t</m> terms
    will always cancel when we are in the case of duplication, for exactly
    this reason. So in practice, once you compute <m>y_p''</m>, you can
    immediately drop the <m>t</m> terms and just work with what remains.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Recognizing duplication:</alert> Check whether <m>k^2</m>
          matches the coefficient of <m>y</m> in the ODE. If so, use the
          modified guess <m>y_p = t(A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt))</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The <m>t</m> terms always cancel:</alert> When substituting
          the modified guess into the ODE, the terms with a factor of <m>t</m>
          will always cancel out. You are left with only plain
          <m>\cos(kt)</m> and <m>\sin(kt)</m> terms to match against the
          right-hand side.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

